## Problem

`src/rules/no-outline-none/index.js:20-25` only recognises the longhand `outline` property with the exact values `0` or `none`:
```js
o.prop.toLowerCase() === 'outline' && ['0', 'none'].indexOf(o.value.toLowerCase()) >= 0
```
Every other spelling of "remove the focus ring" passes.

## Evidence

```
REPORTED  a:focus { outline: none; }
SILENT    a:focus { outline: 0px; }
SILENT    a:focus { outline-style: none; }
```
(`stylelint.lint` with `a11y/no-outline-none: true`.) `outline: 0px`, `outline: 0 none`, `outline-width: 0` and `outline-style: none` all remove the indicator and all pass.

## Impact

This rule is one of three in `recommended.js`. A codebase that uses `outline: 0px` — a very common idiom, and what several popular CSS resets emit — gets a clean report while every keyboard user loses the focus indicator (WCAG 2.4.7).

## Fix

Match the property family and normalise zero values in `src/rules/no-outline-none/index.js`:
```js
const OUTLINE_PROPS = new Set(['outline', 'outline-style', 'outline-width']);
const isZeroOutline = (prop, value) => {
  const v = value.toLowerCase().trim();
  if (prop === 'outline-style') return v === 'none';
  if (prop === 'outline-width') return parseFloat(v) === 0;
  return v === 'none' || parseFloat(v) === 0 || /(^|\s)(none|0(\D|$))/.test(v);
};
const hasEmptyOutline = node.nodes.some(
  (o) => o.type === 'decl' && OUTLINE_PROPS.has(o.prop.toLowerCase()) && isZeroOutline(o.prop.toLowerCase(), o.value)
);
```
Add reject cases for `outline: 0px`, `outline-style: none`, `outline-width: 0` to `src/rules/no-outline-none/__tests__/index.js`.

## Resolution
no-outline-none now matches outline/outline-style/outline-width and any zero-length value. Verified: outline:0px, outline-style:none, outline-width:0 all reported; visible outlines still accepted.

## Problem

`src/rules/no-obsolete-attribute/index.js:17-19` and `src/rules/no-obsolete-element/index.js:16-18` test each comma-separated selector against a `Set` with `Set.has(sel)` — an exact string equality check on the *entire* compound selector. The sets hold bare tokens (`'font'`, `'marquee'`) and bare simple selectors (`'a[charset]'`, `'img[name]'`). Any descendant combinator, extra class, pseudo-class, or attribute value defeats the lookup.

## Evidence

```
REPORTED  a[charset] { color: red; }
SILENT    .x a[charset] { color: red; }
SILENT    a[charset="utf-8"] { color: red; }
REPORTED  font { color: red; }
SILENT    .x font { color: red; }
```
The rules only fire when the stylesheet happens to target the obsolete element or attribute with no context at all — the rarest form in real CSS.

## Impact

Both rules give a false sense of coverage. A stylesheet full of `.header font`, `nav marquee`, `form a[name]` reports clean. Because they never throw and never warn, there is no signal that the check was skipped.

## Fix

Parse the selector instead of string-matching it. `postcss-selector-parser` is already a transitive dependency of stylelint; walk each selector and test the individual tag/attribute nodes:
```js
import parser from 'postcss-selector-parser';
const hasObsolete = (selector, set) => {
  let found = false;
  parser((sel) => sel.walk((n) => {
    if (n.type === 'tag' && set.has(n.value)) found = true;
    if (n.type === 'attribute') {
      const tag = n.parent.nodes.find((s) => s.type === 'tag');
      const key = `${tag ? tag.value : ''}[${n.attribute}]`;
      if (set.has(key) || set.has(`[${n.attribute}]`)) found = true;
    }
  })).processSync(selector);
  return found;
};
```
Add reject cases for `.x font`, `a[charset="utf-8"]` and `.x a[charset]` to both `__tests__/index.js` files.

## Resolution
Both rules now parse selectors via postcss-selector-parser (added as a real dependency, was a phantom). Verified: .x font, a[charset="utf-8"], .x a[charset] all reported; .blink and [data-name] still accepted.

## Problem

`src/rules/no-spread-text/index.js:38-46` handles the `minWidth > maxWidth` case by calling `utils.report()` with an ad-hoc string on `node: root`, rather than by failing `utils.validateOptions()`.

## Evidence

```
$ stylelint.lint({ code: '.a { color: red; }',
    config: { rules: { 'a11y/no-spread-text': [true, { minWidth: 90, maxWidth: 10 }] } } })
REPORTED :: Invalid options: minWidth (90) must not be greater than maxWidth (10) (a11y/no-spread-text)
```
The message is not one of `messages.*`, so it cannot be customised via the `message` secondary option and does not carry the `expected(...)` shape the rest of the plugin uses. It is emitted once per linted file, at position 1:1 of an innocent stylesheet.

## Impact

A configuration error is reported as if the *stylesheet* were at fault, in every file, with a severity and position that point at the wrong thing. It is also suppressible by a `/* stylelint-disable */` comment in the CSS, which silently restores the broken configuration.

## Fix

Express the constraint in the `validateOptions` call at `src/rules/no-spread-text/index.js:17`, which already runs and already knows both values:
```js
const validOptions = utils.validateOptions(result, ruleName, { actual }, {
  actual: options,
  possible: {
    minWidth: [(v) => typeof v === 'number'],
    maxWidth: [(v) => typeof v === 'number'],
  },
  optional: true,
});
if (!validOptions || !actual) return;
if ((options?.minWidth ?? DEFAULT_MIN_WIDTH) > (options?.maxWidth ?? DEFAULT_MAX_WIDTH)) {
  result.warn(`Invalid option: minWidth must not be greater than maxWidth for rule "${ruleName}"`,
    { stylelintType: 'invalidOption' });
  return;
}
```
`stylelintType: 'invalidOption'` makes stylelint treat it as a config error, not a CSS violation. Update the test at `src/rules/no-spread-text/__tests__/index.js` accordingly.

## Resolution
Now uses result.warn with stylelintType 'invalidOption' instead of utils.report on root, so it cannot be silenced by stylelint-disable.

## Problem

`src/rules/font-size-is-readable/index.js:80-86` only inspects declarations whose property is exactly `font-size`, and only values ending in `px`, `pt` or `rem` (`:63-68`). The `font` shorthand and `em`/`%` sizes are invisible to it.

## Evidence

```
SILENT  .a { font: 10px/1.5 Arial; }
SILENT  .a { font-size: 0.5em; }
```
Both are well under the documented 15px floor (`README.md`: "Disallow font sizes less than `15px`"; `src/rules/font-size-is-readable/README.md:36`: "`minSize` (default: `\"15px\"`)").

## Impact

The rule reports clean on two of the most common ways to set a too-small font. `font: 10px/1.5 Arial` in particular is a normal shorthand in resets and component libraries.

## Fix

Handle the shorthand and relative units in `src/rules/font-size-is-readable/index.js`:
```js
const FONT_SIZE_IN_SHORTHAND = /(?:^|\s)((?:\d*\.)?\d+)(px|pt|rem)(?=\s|\/|$)/i;
const sizeOf = (decl) => {
  const prop = decl.prop.toLowerCase();
  if (prop === 'font-size') return decl.value;
  if (prop !== 'font') return null;
  const m = decl.value.match(FONT_SIZE_IN_SHORTHAND);
  return m ? m[1] + m[2] : null;
};
```
then run the existing `checkInPx`/`checkInPt`/`checkInRem` over `sizeOf(o)`. `em` and `%` resolve against an unknown inherited size and cannot be judged in isolation — state that explicitly in `src/rules/font-size-is-readable/README.md` under a "Limitations" heading rather than leaving it implied. Add reject cases for `font: 10px/1.5 Arial` and an accept case for `font: 16px/1.5 Arial`.

## Resolution
Now reads the size component of the font shorthand. em/% documented as out of scope with the reason. Verified: font: 10px/1.5 Arial is reported.

## Problem

`src/rules/line-height-is-vertical-rhythmed/index.js:39-44` recognises exactly two value shapes: a value ending in `px`, and a bare number (`!isNaN(o.value)`). Percentages and `em` values match neither and fall through as accepted.

## Evidence

```
SILENT  .a { line-height: 110%; }
```
`110%` is equivalent to the unitless `1.1`, which the rule *does* report (`minUnitless` default `1.5`, `src/rules/line-height-is-vertical-rhythmed/README.md:57`). The same declared line-height is reported or ignored depending only on how it is spelled.

## Impact

Inconsistent enforcement of a documented threshold. Authors who write `line-height: 110%` or `1.1em` get no warning while authors who write `1.1` do — and switching notation is a silent way to suppress the rule.

## Fix

Normalise percentage and `em` values to the unitless comparison in `src/rules/line-height-is-vertical-rhythmed/index.js`, since both are relative to the element's own font size just as a unitless value is:
```js
const checkInRel = (o) => {
  const v = o.value.toLowerCase().trim();
  if (v.endsWith('%')) return parseFloat(v) / 100 < minUnitless;
  if (v.endsWith('em')) return parseFloat(v) < minUnitless;
  return !isNaN(v) && parseFloat(v) < minUnitless;
};
```
Add reject cases for `110%` and `1.1em` and accept cases for `160%` and `1.6em`.

## Resolution
% and em now normalised to the unitless comparison; rem excluded with a comment (root-relative, not a ratio). Verified: 110% reported, calc() and 1rem skipped.

## Problem

`src/rules/no-spread-text/index.js:64` requires the `max-width` value to end in `ch`:
```js
o.value.toLowerCase().endsWith('ch') && (parseFloat(o.value) < minWidth || parseFloat(o.value) > maxWidth)
```
Every other length unit is skipped.

## Evidence

```
SILENT  .a { color: red; max-width: 1200px; }
```
with `a11y/no-spread-text: true`. 1200px of running text is roughly 150 characters per line at a 16px body font — far outside the documented 45–80 range (`src/rules/no-spread-text/README.md:52-56`) and a clear WCAG 1.4.8 concern.

## Impact

The rule fires only for authors who already express line length in `ch` — that is, authors already thinking about measure. The population the rule exists to catch (`max-width: 1200px` on a text container) is exactly the population it never fires for.

## Fix

Convert the common absolute and font-relative units to an approximate character count before comparing, in `src/rules/no-spread-text/index.js`:
```js
// ponytail: 0.5em per character is the usual average-glyph-width heuristic; good enough for a lint threshold
const CH_PER_EM = 2;
const toCh = (value) => {
  const v = value.toLowerCase().trim();
  const n = parseFloat(v);
  if (!Number.isFinite(n)) return null;
  if (v.endsWith('ch')) return n;
  if (v.endsWith('em') || v.endsWith('rem')) return n * CH_PER_EM;
  if (v.endsWith('px')) return (n / 16) * CH_PER_EM;
  return null;   // %, vw, calc() cannot be resolved statically
};
```
then compare `toCh(o.value)` against `minWidth`/`maxWidth`, skipping `null`. Document the conversion assumption in `src/rules/no-spread-text/README.md`. Add a reject case for `max-width: 1200px` and an accept case for `max-width: 65ch`.

## Resolution
max-width converted to an approximate character count for ch/px/em/rem; unresolvable values skipped. Verified: 1200px reported, 640px accepted, % and calc() skipped.

## Problem

`src/rules/media-prefers-reduced-motion/index.js:140` runs a full-document at-rule scan inside the per-node walker:

    root.walk((node) => {
      ...
      if (context.fix && !isAccepted) {
        let existingMedia = null;
        node.root().walkAtRules('media', (atRule) => {
          if (atRule.params.indexOf('prefers-reduced-motion') >= 0) existingMedia = atRule;
        });

The outer `root.walk` already visits every node once; this adds a second full traversal per violation. The callback also does not stop at the first match — it keeps assigning, so it always walks to the end and always selects the *last* matching media block in the file regardless of where `node` is.

## Evidence

For a stylesheet with `v` violations and `n` at-rules the fix path is O(v·n) rather than O(n). On a design-system stylesheet where most rules animate, `v` scales with `n`, giving quadratic behaviour on the exact input the rule targets. The "last match wins" behaviour is also why fixes accumulate into whichever reduced-motion block happens to appear last, rather than the nearest one.

## Impact

`stylelint --fix` slowdown proportional to the square of stylesheet size, plus fixes landing in an arbitrary distant media block. Neither is visible on the small fixtures in `__tests__/`, so no existing test would catch a regression here.

## Fix

Hoist the scan out of the walker — the set of reduced-motion media blocks does not change per violation, and the one you want is the nearest preceding sibling, not the last in the file:

    const reducedMotionBlocks = [];
    root.walkAtRules('media', (atRule) => {
      if (atRule.params.indexOf('prefers-reduced-motion') >= 0) reducedMotionBlocks.push(atRule);
    });

then inside the walker pick the block sharing `node.parent`, falling back to creating one. Note this interacts with the cascade-ordering defect filed separately — fix that one first, since it changes where the block must go.

## Resolution
Full-tree rescan removed; the fix now searches only the siblings after the node, which is also what the cascade requires.

## Problem

coverage-hole. `addOverrides` has three arms. Two are pinned by tests; the
third -- a later `prefers-reduced-motion` block exists but contains no rule
matching the offending selector -- has no test at all. It is the only
uncovered statement range in the file.

## Evidence

`src/rules/media-prefers-reduced-motion/index.js:162-168` is reported
uncovered by istanbul (`.coverage/coverage-final.json`):

    const override = node.clone();
    override.raws = { ...override.raws, before: '\n', after: '\n', semicolon: true };
    override.removeAll();
    applyOverrides(override, uncovered, node);
    target.append(override);

Every `reject` case in the test file either has no reduced-motion block at all
(`addOverrides` takes the `!target` arm at :133) or has one already containing
the same selector (the `existing` arm at :156).

The path is reachable and its output is correct today, verified with
`stylelint.lint({ fix: true })`:

    input:  a { animation: 1s x; }
            @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { b { animation: none; } }
    output: a { animation: 1s x; }
            @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { b { animation: none; }
            a { animation: none;
            } }

## Impact

This is a `--fix` writer: it mutates the user's stylesheet. A regression here
emits malformed or wrongly-nested CSS, or silently drops the override, and no
test fails. Because the rule is in the recommended config and fixable, the
damage lands in consumer source files on `stylelint --fix`.

## Fix

Add a `reject` case with `fix: true` to
`src/rules/media-prefers-reduced-motion/__tests__/index.js`:

    { code: 'a { animation: 1s x; }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { b { animation: none; } }\n',
      fixed: 'a { animation: 1s x; }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { b { animation: none; }\na { animation: none;\n} }\n',
      message: messages.expected('a'),
      description: 'appends a new rule to an existing reduced-motion block that lacks this selector' }

## Resolution
Added a fix:true reject case pinning the exact output when a later reduced-motion block lacks the selector. Proof: rewriting target.append(override) to node.parent.append(override) now fails the suite; lines 162-168 are covered.

## Problem

`overriddenProperties` (line 46) iterates siblings and, for each child,
tests `if (isReducedMotion(child)) collectOverrides(child.nodes, overridden)`
— without checking that `sibling` is the node under inspection. A nested
`@media (prefers-reduced-motion)` inside *any* later sibling rule therefore
contributes its overrides to the set used to clear the current rule.

The sibling-form branch immediately below it (line 58) does compare
`child.selector === selector`. The nested-form branch has no equivalent
guard.

## Evidence

Probe run against `src/index.js`:

```css
.a { animation: spin 1s; }
.b { @media (prefers-reduced-motion) { animation: none; } }
```
`a11y/media-prefers-reduced-motion` → **silent**.

`.a` has no reduced-motion override at all; `.b`'s override is what silences
it. A user with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` still sees `.a` spin.

## Impact

False negative. One rule anywhere in the file that happens to use the nested
override form disables the check for every animated rule after it — the more
correctly-written the stylesheet, the more defects it hides.

## Fix

Guard the nested branch with the same identity check the flat branch has:

```js
for (const sibling of siblings.slice(index)) {
  if (!sibling || !sibling.nodes) continue;
  for (const child of sibling.nodes) {
    if (sibling === node && isReducedMotion(child)) {
      collectOverrides(child.nodes, overridden);
      continue;
    }
    if (isReducedMotion(sibling) && child.type === 'rule' && child.selector === selector) {
      collectOverrides(child.nodes, overridden);
    }
  }
}
```

Add a reject test for the case above to
`src/rules/media-prefers-reduced-motion/__tests__/index.js`.

## Resolution
overriddenProperties now takes the node and guards the nested branch with sibling === node, so a nested block only covers the rule it is nested in. Reject test added; probe confirms .a is reported when only .b carries a nested override.
